# Line endings: LF, everywhere, for every text file.
#
# This repo is developed on Linux and runs in Linux containers, so LF is the
# only ending that is correct at the point of use. CRLF has already cost us
# real bugs here:
#
#   * A YAML saved with CRLF injects a stray carriage return into the values
#     that docker_setup/*.sh parse for `output_dir` / `synthpop_dir`, which
#     makes `docker run --mount` fail with "value should not have whitespace".
#     (The scripts also strip \r defensively, but keeping tracked files LF
#     avoids the problem at the source.)
#   * A shell script with CRLF has a shebang of `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which
#     fails as `bad interpreter: ... ^M`.
#   * A Dockerfile with CRLF can break RUN line continuations.
#   * Worst of all, a blob committed WITH CRLF while an attribute claims
#     eol=lf is permanently unstageable-clean: eol=lf governs only checkout, so
#     the CRs survive it, while the clean filter strips them on staging. git
#     then reports the file as modified in every worktree forever, including a
#     pristine checkout -- which makes `git rebase` and `git merge` refuse to
#     run, and mid-rebase presents as commits mysteriously "becoming empty".
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide text-vs-binary by its own NUL-byte heuristic and
# normalise only what is text; `eol=lf` then checks text out as LF on every
# platform, including Windows. Modern Windows editors (VS Code, RStudio,
# Notepad since 2018) all handle LF, so this costs Windows users nothing.
#
# This is deliberately a wildcard rather than the extension whitelist it
# replaces: the whitelist covered *.sh/*.yaml/*.yml/*.R/*.ps1 and therefore
# silently ignored LICENSE, docker_setup/Dockerfile.*, *.csv, and every file
# type nobody had thought of yet.
#
# Audit with: auxil/audit_line_endings.py   (inspects blobs, not the worktree)

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise. `text=auto` already classifies these correctly by
# content, but naming them is free insurance against a file that happens to
# start with 8000 NUL-free bytes.
*.docx binary
*.jpeg binary
*.jpg  binary
*.png  binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xls  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.bz2  binary
*.7z   binary
*.so   binary
*.o    binary
*.dll  binary
*.dylib binary

# Model data formats. Not tracked today -- they are gitignored and come from
# Zenodo -- but named so that accidentally `git add`ing one cannot corrupt it.
*.fst     binary
*.parquet binary
*.rds     binary
*.RData   binary
*.rda     binary
*.qs      binary
*.feather binary
*.arrow   binary
